findings.body:
The new `clusterDeleteKeysInUnownedSlots()` runs before `clusterClaimUnassignedSlots()` inside `verifyClusterConfigWithData()`. Its skip condition is `clusterIsMySlot(i) || getImportingSlotSource(i)`. For a slot with no owner, `clusterIsMySlot()` returns false, because `getNodeBySlot()` returns NULL. `getImportingSlotSource()` also returns NULL. So every populated slot with no owner is treated as foreign, and `clusterDelKeysInSlot()` drops its keys. By the time the claim pass runs, those keys are gone, so the claim branch never fires for any slot that had data.

The original single loop handled this correctly. For each populated slot it either claimed the slot when `slots[j] == NULL`, or deleted the keys when another node owned it. The split loses that distinction. The result is that a node restarting with keys in an unassigned slot silently loses that data. The log line "assigned to another node" is also wrong for this case.

The PR description says there are no behavioral changes, so this would be an unintended regression.

::code{file="src/cluster.c" lines="2146-2169"}

::code{file="src/cluster.c" lines="2208-2213"}

Running the claim pass first fixes the ordering. After it, any populated slot that was unowned is owned by `myself`, so the delete pass skips it. Adding a `serverLog` assertion or a test for "keys in unassigned slot survive restart" would guard against this recurring. I did not find an existing test that covers this path in `tests/unit/cluster/`.

::suggestion{file="src/cluster.c" lines="2210-2212"}
    /* Take over slots that we have keys for, but are assigned to no one.
     * This must run before deleting keys in slots owned by other nodes,
     * otherwise unowned slots would be wrongly treated as foreign. */
    clusterClaimUnassignedSlots();
    /* Delete keys in slots that are assigned to other nodes. */
    clusterDeleteKeysInUnownedSlots();

`clusterCommonInit()` now allocates the per-slot stats array with plain `malloc()`. Before this PR, `main()` used `zmalloc()`. Two things change as a result:

- The array is no longer counted in `zmalloc_used_memory()`, so `INFO memory` and `maxmemory` accounting drop about 48 bytes per slot (roughly 786 KB across 16384 slots) without any note.
- `malloc()` can return NULL, and there is no check. `zmalloc()` aborts on out-of-memory. With `malloc()`, the first `server.cluster_slot_stats[slot]` write is a NULL dereference, and `resetClusterStats()` would `memset` NULL. The crash would show up far from the allocation.

The PR description calls this a pure refactor. This is an allocator change, and it is not needed for the move.

::diff{file="src/cluster.c" lines="2137-2141"}

Use `zmalloc()` to match the rest of the codebase and the previous behavior.

::suggestion{file="src/cluster.c" lines="2138-2138"}
    server.cluster_slot_stats = zmalloc(CLUSTER_SLOTS*sizeof(clusterSlotStat));
